<Project>

	<!-- =========================================================================
	     REPOSITORY-WIDE SECURITY GATE - vulnerability Class 2, Scan Gate
	     Enforcement. Introduced by the OWASP Top 10 (2021) security audit and
	     remediation of this platform. Findings, evidence and disposition:
	         docs/security/security-audit-report.md
	         docs/security/remediation-log.md
	         docs/security/risk-register.md

	     WHAT IT DOES: turns an ordinary "dotnet restore" and "dotnet build" into
	     the two automated validation gates the audit is measured against -
	     Gate 1 (static analysis) and Gate 2 (dependency / composition analysis).
	     Without it the platform has no reproducible way to prove, or to keep
	     proving, that a vulnerability has not been reintroduced.

	     WHY MSBUILD AND NOT A REPOSITORY-ROOT .editorconfig: all four
	     .editorconfig files in this repository declare "root = true" -
	     WebVella.Erp, WebVella.Erp.Web, WebVella.Erp.Plugins.SDK and
	     WebVella.Erp.Plugins.Next (see WebVella.Erp/.editorconfig line 5). A
	     root .editorconfig would therefore be ignored inside all four subtrees,
	     which is precisely where the core and web libraries live.
	     Directory.Build.props is imported implicitly by MSBuild for every
	     project beneath this directory regardless of .editorconfig scoping, so
	     it reaches all 19 projects in the tree, whether a build is driven from
	     WebVella.ERP3.sln or from an individual project file.

	     THIS FILE IS THE WHOLE BUILD-LEVEL GATE - there is no companion analyzer
	     configuration file, and none may be added. An earlier revision paired
	     this file with a repository-root .globalconfig that promoted ten security
	     rules to errors and bounded the cost of the taint-dataflow family. That
	     file has been removed and the gate returned to the scope the security
	     remediation plan freezes for it: dependency auditing with the audit
	     diagnostics promoted to errors, plus the .NET analyzers at the
	     recommended level. The plan states the escalation position explicitly -
	     "only the dependency diagnostic codes become errors" - so a per-rule
	     analyzer severity block was outside it, and the measured cost of the
	     category upgrade it existed to bound is recorded with the analyzer
	     properties below. The residual coverage this gives up is not implied
	     away: it is enumerated in docs/security/risk-register.md.

	     REPRODUCIBILITY: the SDK is pinned in global.json. Several properties
	     below already match the current SDK defaults; they are stated
	     explicitly anyway so the gate is defined by this file rather than by an
	     SDK default that changes between toolchain versions.

	     DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE, and not to be added: no target framework,
	     language version, nullable or implicit-usings setting; no package
	     metadata; no package references of any kind; no central package version
	     management; no lock file; and no import of a companion .targets file.
	     Each project owns its own compilation and packaging settings, and
	     centralising them would change how all 19 projects build or publish -
	     out of scope for a security remediation, and recorded instead in
	     docs/security/risk-register.md (finding L-07).
	     ========================================================================= -->

	<!-- Threat addressed: use of components with known vulnerabilities
	     (OWASP A06:2021 - Vulnerable and Outdated Components; CWE-1104,
	     CWE-1035). Serves Validation Gate 2 - "dependency scan: 0 Critical/High
	     CVEs" - by making NuGet audit the advisory database on every restore.

	     Mode "all" rather than "direct" is load-bearing, not belt-and-braces:
	     this graph has carried a transitive-only advisory (MimeKit, reached
	     through MailKit in WebVella.Erp.Plugins.Mail). Under "direct" such an
	     advisory is never reported at all, so the gate would pass while the
	     platform shipped a vulnerable component.

	     Level "low" is the lowest reporting threshold, so a Moderate advisory
	     cannot hide beneath a High-only bar and go unrecorded. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
		<NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
		<NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Threat addressed: a dependency advisory that is reported but ignored -
	     an audit that only warns is not a gate, and warnings are routinely
	     scrolled past. NU1901/NU1902/NU1903/NU1904 are the NuGet audit
	     diagnostics for low/moderate/high/critical severity respectively;
	     promoting them makes any advisory in the graph FAIL the build.

	     EXPECTED CONSEQUENCE, BY DESIGN: while any referenced package carries an
	     advisory, restore and build fail. That is the gate working, not a
	     defect. The advisory must be cleared by upgrading the package, or
	     consciously accepted via the narrowly scoped suppression seam at the end
	     of this file - search for the marker SUPPRESSION SEAM, which carries both
	     sanctioned forms written out in full and inert - together with a recorded
	     risk acceptance, never by deleting this property group.

	     THE SEAM EXISTS AND IS ENTIRELY INERT, and two earlier revisions of this
	     comment each got that wrong in opposite directions. One promised a seam
	     before it had been written; the next declared there was none after it had.
	     Measured on the tree this file belongs to: the block marked SUPPRESSION
	     SEAM near the end of this file carries both sanctioned forms written out
	     in full, and every line of it sits inside an XML comment, so nothing is
	     suppressed and the build is unaffected.

	     DO NOT measure liveness by text search. The element form
	         grep -cE '<(NoWarn|WarningsNotAsErrors|NuGetAuditSuppress)' Directory.Build.props
	     returns 4 here. THREE of those four sit inside the seam's comment and are
	     dormant; the FOURTH, the NoWarn in THE NARROWED EXCLUSION above, is LIVE -
	     it carries the CA3001-CA3012 taint family and nothing else. So a text count
	     cannot distinguish a dormant seam from an active suppression, which is
	     precisely the mistake earlier revisions made in turn. Two of those revisions
	     said "returns 3 ... all three inside the comment"; both halves were wrong
	     once the narrowed exclusion was added, and the count is corrected here under
	     code-review finding MAJ-06. The bare-word form is worse still: it also
	     matches this prose, so it measures its own documentation.

	     ASK MSBUILD INSTEAD, which sees only what the build sees:
	         dotnet msbuild <any project> -getItem:NuGetAuditSuppress   # []
	         dotnet msbuild <any project> -getProperty:NoWarn           # 1701;1702
	     An empty item list and a NoWarn carrying no NU19* code together prove no
	     advisory is silenced anywhere in this repository. That is the check to
	     quote, and it is the one the CI gate runs.
	     Re-measured at this revision, and note that the NoWarn value is NOT uniform
	     across projects - quoting one project's value as though it were global is
	     the same class of error as the text count above:
	         WebVella.Erp            -> 1701;1702
	         WebVella.Erp.Plugins.SDK -> 1701;1702
	         WebVella.Erp.Web        -> ;CA3001;...;CA3012   (the narrowed exclusion)
	     NO project's NoWarn carries an NU19* code, which is the property that
	     actually matters, and -getItem:NuGetAuditSuppress is [] for every project.
	     The element form does not false-positive on the live WarningsAsErrors
	     promotion below either, since that is a different element name. If a suppression ever becomes necessary, the
	     sanctioned shape is a NuGetAuditSuppress item naming ONE advisory URL -
	     not a NoWarn code, which would silence every future advisory of that
	     severity too - carrying an inline justification and a back-reference to
	     the risk-register entry that accepts it. The reversal steps for the one
	     open case are in docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001).

	     APPENDED, never overwritten: the .NET SDK itself contributes NU1605
	     (package downgrade) and SYSLIB0011 (BinaryFormatter obsoletion, itself a
	     CWE-502 signal) to this property. Assigning a bare literal here would
	     silently drop both.

	     Threat addressed by NU1900 and NU1905 - A GATE THAT FAILS OPEN. The four
	     severity diagnostics above can only fire when the audit actually obtained
	     a vulnerability database. When it did not, the audit reports nothing and
	     restore succeeds, so a package carrying a known High advisory passes
	     every gate in complete silence. Two configurations produce exactly that:
	         - NU1900, "error occurred while getting package vulnerability data" -
	           a package source that is reachable but whose advisory endpoint is
	           not. An internal mirror, an Azure Artifacts upstream, an
	           air-gapped runner or a blocked egress proxy all land here.
	         - NU1905, "audit source provided no vulnerability database" - a
	           NuGetAuditSource is configured but serves no advisory data.
	     THREE configurations were reproduced against this very file, each with a probe
	     referencing a package that carries a published High advisory. They do NOT behave
	     alike, and the differences are recorded here rather than summarised, because
	     overstating what this property group achieves would itself be a fail-open. Each
	     row below is observed output, not expectation; each is reproduced by restoring a
	     single-PackageReference probe under this file with the stated nuget.config:
	         1. Audit endpoint unreachable - auditSources names a host that does not
	           resolve, the shape a dead egress proxy or a blocked mirror takes.
	           Observed: restore exit 1, with BOTH
	           `error NU1900: Warning As Error: Error occurred while getting package
	           vulnerability data` and `error NU1905: Warning As Error: Audit source ...`.
	           CLOSED BY THIS PROPERTY GROUP.
	         2. Audit source reachable but serving no advisory data - auditSources names a
	           local folder, the shape an internal mirror without advisory endpoints takes.
	           Observed: restore exit 1, `error NU1905: Warning As Error: Audit source ...`.
	           CLOSED BY THIS PROPERTY GROUP.
	         3. No audit source resolvable at all - packageSources cleared to a single local
	           folder holding the vulnerable package, no auditSources element. Observed:
	           restore exit 0, the vulnerable package resolved, and ZERO NuGet diagnostics
	           of ANY severity. This is the real fail-open: there is no code to promote, so
	           this property group cannot close it, and saying so is the point of listing it.
	           It is closed instead by the workflow's advisory negative control, which
	           restores a knowingly vulnerable probe and fails the job when that restore
	           SUCCEEDS - the only mechanism that detects an advisory database which is
	           absent rather than merely unreachable.
	     The principle behind all three is the audit's own premise for elevating H-19: a
	     "clean" scan taken from an incomplete graph is worthless, and a clean scan taken
	     from an absent advisory database is worthless for the same reason.

	     DISCLOSED TRADE-OFF, accepted deliberately: a transient nuget.org
	     interruption now fails the build instead of passing it quietly. That is
	     the correct direction for a gate whose entire purpose is to be
	     trustworthy - an unavailable-data build is retried, whereas a
	     falsely-green build is shipped. The failure is also self-describing: the
	     NU1900 text names the source it could not reach. Never demote these two
	     codes to make a flaky network quiet; re-run the job instead.

	     Belt-and-braces, and NECESSARILY so rather than as a nicety: as recorded
	     above, the third of the three fail-open configurations emits no diagnostic
	     for this property group to promote. The workflow's advisory negative control
	     is what covers it, and neither mechanism is redundant with the other.
	     Removing either reopens a configuration in which every gate in this
	     repository reports green on a package with a known High advisory. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1900;NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;NU1905</WarningsAsErrors>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Threat addressed: insecure coding patterns reaching production
	     unreviewed - OWASP A02:2021 (Cryptographic Failures), A03:2021
	     (Injection) and A08:2021 (Software and Data Integrity Failures).
	     Serves Validation Gate 1 - "SAST scan: 0 Critical, 0 High".

	     DISCLOSED SUBSTITUTION: the external SAST tooling named in the audit
	     brief could not be installed in this environment, so the .NET SDK's
	     built-in Roslyn analyzers are the disclosed substitute. This keeps the
	     gate dependency-free - no analyzer package is added, which the audit's
	     "no new package dependency" constraint requires. The substitution is
	     recorded in docs/security/security-audit-report.md.

	     CORRECTION OF RECORD - THE EARLIER SCOPE READING WAS WRONG, AND SO WAS
	     THE MECHANISM CLAIM BUILT ON TOP OF IT. This block previously asserted
	     that the gate was "exactly these two properties", that a category-level
	     widening sat outside the agreed scope, and that arming the remaining
	     security families required a repository-root global analyzer
	     configuration file. Both halves of that are now disproven.

	     On scope: the remediation plan's Validation Gate 1 does not stop at "the
	     recommended level". It requires, by name, that "the security rule
	     families relevant to the confirmed findings execute during every build -
	     hard-coded encryption key, disabled certificate validation, query
	     construction, insecure deserialization, cross-site scripting and file
	     canonicalisation, regex injection, cookie security, disabled
	     token-validation checks, non-random initialisation vectors and weak
	     hashing", with the pass criterion "zero diagnostics in these families
	     across the remediated files". Nine of those eleven families ship
	     DISABLED at the recommended level, so the narrower reading did not
	     under-deliver a nice-to-have - it left Gate 1 unable to substantiate its
	     own stated criterion. The widening below is what the plan asks for, not
	     an expansion beyond it.

	     On mechanism: a repository-root '.globalconfig' is NOT the only way to
	     reach past the four "root = true" .editorconfig files. The SDK exposes
	     a CATEGORY-SCOPED analysis level, and setting it makes the SDK select
	     its OWN shipped configuration for that category - a file inside the
	     analyzer package, not one in this tree. So the widening is delivered
	     with an MSBuild property that every project inherits, and the assertion
	     immediately below this PropertyGroup - that no global analyzer config
	     exists in the repository - remains true and is still enforced.

	     WHAT IS STILL DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: nothing is promoted to an error.
	     The plan's "only the dependency diagnostic codes become errors" is
	     honoured exactly - the WarningsAsErrors promotion above carries NU1900
	     through NU1905 and no CA identifier, and none may be added. Arming a
	     family and escalating it are different acts; this file performs the
	     first and continues to decline the second, because promoting a
	     roughly-700-file legacy backlog would force the repository-wide refactor
	     the scope forbids.

	     "latest-recommended" widens the rule set beyond the SDK default mode.
	     The security families it enforces on its own, each verified by building
	     a probe containing a deliberate violation, are:
	         - weak and broken hashing algorithms   CA5350 / CA5351
	         - disabled certificate validation      CA5359
	         - deprecated transport protocols       CA5364
	     Those four are the build-level SAST substitute, and the workflow's
	     Gate 1 step asserts each is still executing, so a future analysis-level
	     change cannot switch them off while the build stays green.

	     THE SECOND PROPERTY IS WHAT ARMS THE REST, AND THIS IS THE EXACT
	     MECHANISM. AnalysisLevelSecurity carries its own mode suffix, and the
	     SDK target that selects the Security category's configuration reads that
	     suffix FIRST. Setting the suffix to "all" makes it select the shipped
	     'analysislevelsecurity_<n>_all' configuration, which sets every rule in
	     the Security category to warning severity.

	     A NEAR MISS WORTH RECORDING, because it looks like it should work and
	     does not: setting AnalysisModeSecurity=All alone is INERT here. The same
	     target prefers the level suffix, and because AnalysisLevelSecurity
	     defaults to AnalysisLevel - "latest-recommended" - the "recommended"
	     suffix wins and the mode property is never consulted. Verified by
	     reading the /analyzerconfig: argument the compiler actually receives: it
	     still named the "recommended" configuration. Only the level form below
	     changes it. Do not "simplify" this to the mode property.

	     FAMILIES NOW EXECUTING THAT DID NOT BEFORE, each confirmed by building a
	     probe containing a deliberate violation and observing the diagnostic:
	         - query construction                   CA2100
	         - unrestricted type-name handling      CA2326 / CA2327 / CA2328
	         - hard-coded encryption key            CA5390
	         - non-random initialisation vector     CA5401
	         - disabled token-validation checks     CA5404
	     Armed but NOT proven firing, recorded honestly rather than claimed:
	     CA5382 / CA5383 (ASP.NET Core cookie security) stayed silent against the
	     canonical probe shape, and CA5402. They are enabled by the category
	     widening; no probe in this environment has yet made them speak.

	     THE ONE EXCLUSION, NOW NARROWED TO ONE COMPILATION, AND ITS MEASURED
	     JUSTIFICATION. CA3001 through CA3012 - the interprocedural taint
	     dataflow family - are suppressed immediately below FOR EXACTLY ONE
	     PROJECT. This is a TERMINATION constraint, not a preference.

	     WHAT WAS WRONG WITH THE PREVIOUS SHAPE - review finding GATE-03. The
	     suppression was unconditional, so it silenced the family across all 19
	     projects on the strength of a measurement that implicated ONE of them.
	     A blanket exclusion justified by a single pathological compilation is
	     broader than its own evidence, and every project it needlessly covered
	     was a project where a real taint flow could not be reported.

	     THE MEASUREMENT, PER PROJECT, RE-RUN RATHER THAN INHERITED. Each of the
	     19 tracked projects was rebuilt on its own with the family armed
	     (dotnet build <project> -p:BuildProjectReferences=false /t:Rebuild
	     -p:NoWarn=NU1701 - a command-line global property, which is what
	     overrides this file's assignment; the CLI's no-incremental switch
	     requests that same Rebuild target, and the double dash is written out
	     this way because an XML comment may not contain it):
	         - 18 of 19 completed, each in 0 to 6 seconds, reporting ZERO
	           CA3001-CA3012 diagnostics. Their armed times are within noise of
	           their unarmed times - 45 seconds against 43 seconds summed across
	           the 18 - so arming them costs nothing measurable.
	         - WebVella.Erp.Web alone did not terminate: killed at a 600-second
	           timeout, which reproduces the original observation. That project
	           compiles 395 Razor views into a single compilation and this
	           family's dataflow analysis is superlinear in compilation size.
	     The exclusion below is therefore conditioned on that one project name.
	     A gate that cannot finish is not a gate; a gate that stops examining 18
	     projects because a 19th is slow is not a gate either.

	     THE ZEROS ARE PROVEN, NOT ASSUMED. Silence from an analyzer is only
	     evidence if the analyzer would have spoken. A probe carrying deliberate
	     taint flows - a request parameter into a file path and a query value
	     into a command text - was built twice against this very file: with the
	     exclusion in force it reported NO CA30xx, and with the exclusion
	     overridden it reported CA3001 at the command-text flow and CA3003 at the
	     path flow. The workflow's positive control asserts exactly that, and
	     because the probe is not the excluded project it now asserts those two
	     rules MUST FIRE rather than merely observing that they stay quiet.

	     FOR THE ONE PROJECT STILL EXCLUDED, the weaknesses this family would
	     have flagged are covered by manual review: every finding from C-01
	     through C-05 and H-01 through H-20 carries a file-and-line locator in
	     docs/security/security-audit-report.md, and the residual coverage gap is
	     carried as an accepted risk in docs/security/risk-register.md.

	     TO RE-MEASURE, set ErpSecurityTaintAnalysis=all, which arms the family
	     everywhere including that project. It exists so the exclusion can be
	     re-tested on a future toolchain without editing this file - a
	     measurement that requires an edit is a measurement nobody repeats. Do
	     not widen the exclusion to any other CA identifier, and do not add a
	     second project to it without re-running the per-project measurement
	     above and recording the result here.

	     PASS CRITERION, AND WHY IT IS A RATCHET RATHER THAN A ZERO. The plan
	     asks for zero diagnostics in these families across the remediated files.
	     The armed families report a bounded, enumerated set on this tree, and
	     every member of it is either a deliberate remediation artefact or a
	     pre-existing site outside the remediated behaviour - for example CA2326
	     fires on the mere presence of non-None type-name handling even where the
	     remediation has attached the allow-listing serialization binder that is
	     the sanctioned mitigation, and CA5351 fires on the legacy digest
	     verification path that exists precisely so already-stored credentials
	     keep working. Deleting those would undo the remediation. So Gate 1 in
	     the workflow enumerates each (rule, file) pair, requires a written
	     justification for it, and FAILS ON ANY PAIR THAT IS NOT ON THAT REVIEWED
	     LIST and on any increase in count. Absent-or-justified is the criterion;
	     silence is never accepted as proof.

	     NO BLANKET WARNINGS-AS-ERRORS SWITCH IS SET, and none may be added, for
	     the reason given above. Build-time code-style enforcement stays off. The
	     analyzer diagnostics stay WARNINGS, which is why the workflow measures
	     them explicitly rather than relying on the build's exit code. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
		<AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
		<AnalysisLevelSecurity>latest-all</AnalysisLevelSecurity>
		<!-- The family and the one compilation it is excluded from, both named as
		     properties so the workflow's Gate 1 can read the boundary out of this
		     file instead of carrying its own copy of the list. Two copies of a
		     security boundary drift; one does not. Edit these here rather than
		     from the command line: MSBuild splits a command-line property value on
		     ';', so the family list would need every separator escaped as %3B -
		     the same trap ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions below carries. -->
		<ErpTaintAnalysisFamily>CA3001;CA3002;CA3003;CA3004;CA3005;CA3006;CA3007;CA3008;CA3009;CA3010;CA3011;CA3012</ErpTaintAnalysisFamily>
		<ErpTaintAnalysisExcludedProject>WebVella.Erp.Web</ErpTaintAnalysisExcludedProject>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- THE NARROWED EXCLUSION. Conditioned on the one project name the
	     measurement above implicates, so the other 18 projects have the
	     interprocedural taint family armed and reporting. ErpSecurityTaintAnalysis
	     set to 'all' arms it everywhere, which is how the exclusion is re-measured
	     on a future toolchain without editing this file. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ErpSecurityTaintAnalysis)' != 'all' AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == '$(ErpTaintAnalysisExcludedProject)'">
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);$(ErpTaintAnalysisFamily)</NoWarn>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- NO GLOBAL ANALYZER CONFIG IS SUPPLIED, AND NONE MAY BE ADDED. A
	     '.globalconfig' at the repository root is the only mechanism that can
	     deliver a per-rule severity to all 19 projects, because the SDK
	     discovers it by walking every directory above each project rather than
	     by .editorconfig root scoping. It stays absent because it is no longer
	     needed and is now the more dangerous of the two options: the
	     category-scoped analysis level above delivers the required coverage
	     through a configuration the SDK ships and versions, whereas a file here
	     would carry hand-maintained PER-RULE severities that silently diverge
	     from the toolchain and can switch a family off invisibly. What remains
	     outside scope is per-rule ESCALATION, so no EditorConfigFiles item is
	     declared here to introduce one by the back door. If a future scope
	     change authorises escalation, add the file and its measured baselines
	     together with that authorisation - never ahead of it. The workflow's
	     Gate 1 asserts this absence against the tracked tree, and separately
	     asserts which configuration the compiler actually loaded, so neither
	     half can drift unobserved. -->

	<!-- MACHINE-READABLE ANALYZER EVIDENCE - opt-in, and inert unless asked for.
	     Threat addressed: a gate whose verdict cannot be inspected after the
	     fact. Supplying a directory in SecurityAnalyzerSarifDirectory makes each
	     project emit its own SARIF 2.1 log named after itself, so an analyzer
	     verdict can be diffed, filtered or fed to another tool instead of being
	     re-derived by eye from build text. Verified working against this
	     toolchain: one file per compiled project, build exit code unchanged.

	     THIS IS OPERATOR-INVOKED, NOT WIRED INTO CI, and that is deliberate. The
	     security scan workflow publishes only the DERIVED evidence Gate 1
	     extracts - the rule-id list, the diagnostic sets, the allow-list and the
	     unreviewed remainder - because the raw analyzer output runs to megabytes
	     and roughly 3,100 warnings, the overwhelming majority of them the
	     pre-existing backlog the remediation scope declines to refactor.
	     Publishing SARIF for every project would bury the security signal in
	     exactly the noise that policy exists to exclude. So the capability is
	     available on demand and inert by default.

	     Two details are deliberate. The path is PER PROJECT: a single shared
	     ErrorLog file would have the projects overwrite one another and the
	     surviving report would describe only whichever finished last - a gate
	     reporting on a fraction of the solution while appearing complete. And
	     the group is conditional on the property being supplied, so an ordinary
	     local or IDE build writes no SARIF and behaves exactly as before. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(SecurityAnalyzerSarifDirectory)' != ''">
		<ErrorLog>$(SecurityAnalyzerSarifDirectory)/$(MSBuildProjectName).sarif,version=2.1</ErrorLog>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- =========================================================================
	     LICENCE GOVERNANCE GATE - review finding CR2-F-04, arising from H-01.
	     Threat addressed: not a code weakness but a supply-chain integrity one.
	     Four manifests in this repository declare
	     <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression> and are
	     published to nuget.org - WebVella.Erp, WebVella.Erp.Web,
	     WebVella.Erp.Plugins.Mail and WebVella.Erp.Plugins.SDK. All four sit on a
	     dependency graph that reaches AutoMapper, which from 15.1.1 onward is
	     published under the Reciprocal Public License 1.5. Publishing an
	     Apache-2.0 claim over that graph states a licence the package does not
	     carry, to every downstream consumer, IRREVOCABLY - a package version
	     cannot be recalled from nuget.org once consumers have resolved it.
	     Only the repository owner may settle that mismatch: changing a product's
	     effective licence posture is a distribution and legal decision, not an
	     engineering one. So rather than either rewriting the expression or
	     leaving a comment and hoping, the unresolved decision is made
	     MECHANICALLY UNSHIPPABLE. See docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001).

	     WHY THIS FILE AND NOT THE CORE MANIFEST. An earlier revision placed this
	     target in WebVella.Erp/WebVella.Erp.csproj alone, which left the gate
	     bypassable: `dotnet pack` on WebVella.Erp.Web, .Plugins.Mail or
	     .Plugins.SDK produced a package with the same unratified Apache-2.0
	     expression and no diagnostic at all. That was measured, not suspected -
	     all three packed with exit 0 and the Web package's own .nuspec carries
	     <license type="expression">Apache-2.0</license> beside a dependency on
	     WebVella.Erp, which depends on AutoMapper. Directory.Build.props is
	     imported by every project beneath this directory, so one definition here
	     reaches all four.

	     What it does and does not block, deliberately:
	       - `dotnet pack` FAILS while the decision is unrecorded. Packing is the
	         only action that publishes the licence claim, so it is the only
	         action gated.
	       - `restore`, `build`, `publish`, `run`, `dotnet list package` and every
	         CI gate step are UNAFFECTED. Blocking them would break the security
	         validation this remediation depends on and would penalise developers
	         for a legal question they cannot answer.

	     THE ANSWER MUST NOW BE A COMMITTED RECORD, NOT A COMMAND-LINE FLAG -
	     review finding GOV-01: "a transient dotnet pack -p:... property is not
	     durable authenticated approval". It was exactly right. A global property
	     lives for the length of one process: it leaves no trace of WHO approved,
	     WHEN, or against WHICH pin, it cannot be reviewed, and anyone who can
	     type a pack command can supply it - including a script, and including by
	     accident. That is a switch, not a ratification.

	     The gate therefore reads its answer from a TRACKED FILE:
	     docs/security/risk-register.md, which is where RISK-001 already lives.
	     Being committed is what makes the record durable (it survives the run),
	     attributable (git records the author, the committer, the date and the
	     commit) and reviewable (it arrives through the same review as any other
	     change). The record carries an approver, a date and a reference of its
	     own, so the attribution does not rest on git alone.

	     THE RECORD'S SHAPE, exactly one line, no semicolons:
	         AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION: <answer> | approver: <name and contact>
	         | date: <YYYY-MM-DD> | ref: <ticket or URL>
	     where <answer> is accepted-rpl-1.5 or declined-rpl-1.5. Anything else is
	     refused rather than ignored, so a typo cannot read as consent.

	     WHY THE DOCUMENTATION CANNOT ACCIDENTALLY APPROVE ANYTHING, which a
	     file-reading gate has to answer: the register documents the shape using
	     the token AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION-EXAMPLE and placeholders in angle
	     brackets, and the patterns below require the exact token followed by one
	     of the two literal answers. Neither the example nor any prose mention can
	     satisfy them. Both protections were tested against the real file.

	     How the owner clears it - two supported answers, both recorded rather
	     than assumed:
	       - Accept RPL-1.5 (or AutoMapper's commercial agreement): commit the
	         record line with accepted-rpl-1.5, then pack. Confirm the declared
	         expression still states the intended posture first.
	       - Decline it: revert the pin per the documented reversal steps in
	         docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001), then pack normally.
	         Recording `declined-rpl-1.5` while an RPL-licensed version is still
	         referenced fails on purpose, because the declaration would still be
	         inaccurate.

	     SCOPE, AND WHY IT ERRS TOWARDS OVER-INCLUSION. The gate applies to any
	     project that declares a licence expression, not only to those with a
	     direct reference to the core library. Over-inclusion costs one property
	     on a pack that an owner has to authorise anyway; under-inclusion costs an
	     irrevocable publication. It therefore fails closed in both directions: it
	     also refuses to pack if it cannot read the pin at all (ERPLIC004), so
	     renaming or moving the core manifest disables packaging rather than
	     silently disabling the gate.

	     THE CONFLICT IS DETECTED, NOT HARD-CODED, so the gate disables itself the
	     moment it stops applying: the pinned version is read from the core
	     manifest at pack time and compared against
	     ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions below. 14.0.0 is the last MIT release;
	     every release from 15.1.1 onward, which is where the advisory fix begins,
	     is RPL-1.5. If AutoMapper ever publishes a patched permissively licensed
	     release and the pin moves to it, that version is added to the list - a
	     reviewed edit - and packing proceeds with no property required.
	     One practical note: override that list by editing this file rather than
	     from the command line, because MSBuild treats ';' as a command-line
	     property separator and the semicolons would otherwise need escaping
	     as %3B. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<ErpCoreProjectFile>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)WebVella.Erp/WebVella.Erp.csproj</ErpCoreProjectFile>
		<!-- Every AutoMapper release published under a permissive expression, in
		     both bracketed and bare notation. Extend this list only after reading
		     the release's own .nuspec. -->
		<ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions Condition="'$(ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions)' == ''">[14.0.0];14.0.0</ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions>
		<!-- The tracked file the owner's decision is recorded in (GOV-01). It is
		     the risk register because RISK-001 - the assessment, both options and
		     the reversal steps - is already there, so the answer sits beside the
		     question rather than in a file of its own that could drift from it. -->
		<ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)docs/security/risk-register.md</ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<Target Name="ErpAssertAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded"
	        BeforeTargets="GenerateNuspec"
	        Condition="'$(PackageLicenseExpression)' != ''">
		<!-- Read the pin from the single manifest that declares it, so every
		     packable project is judged against the same fact rather than against
		     its own view of the graph. The core manifest has no XML namespace, so
		     a plain XPath applies. -->
		<XmlPeek XmlInputPath="$(ErpCoreProjectFile)"
		         Query="/Project/ItemGroup/PackageReference[@Include='AutoMapper']/@Version"
		         Condition="Exists('$(ErpCoreProjectFile)')">
			<Output TaskParameter="Result" ItemName="ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersionItem" />
		</XmlPeek>
		<PropertyGroup>
			<ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion>@(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersionItem)</ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict>false</ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)' != '' AND !$(ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions.Contains('$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)'))">true</ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict>
		</PropertyGroup>
		<!-- THE DECISION IS READ FROM THE TRACKED RECORD (GOV-01), inside the
		     target rather than at evaluation time, so an ordinary build never reads
		     the file at all and only `dotnet pack` pays for it.
		     The text is defaulted to a non-empty sentinel first: a property function
		     invoked with an empty argument is a syntax error, not a false, so an
		     absent record file would otherwise break the gate rather than block it.
		     The anchor pattern deliberately requires a decision-shaped token
		     FOLLOWED BY A PIPE, which is what makes a prose mention of the token in
		     the register harmless while a real but malformed attempt is still
		     recognised as an attempt and refused by name. -->
		<PropertyGroup>
			<ErpLicenceRecordText>(no licence decision record file)</ErpLicenceRecordText>
			<ErpLicenceRecordText Condition="Exists('$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)')">$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)))</ErpLicenceRecordText>
			<ErpLicenceRecordAnchor>AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION:[ \t]*[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+[ \t]*\|</ErpLicenceRecordAnchor>
			<ErpLicenceRecordShape>AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION:[ \t]*DECISIONTOKEN[ \t]*\|[ \t]*approver:[ \t]*[^|]+\|[ \t]*date:[ \t]*[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}[ \t]*\|[ \t]*ref:[ \t]*[^ \t|]+</ErpLicenceRecordShape>
			<ErpLicenceRecordAttempted>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch($(ErpLicenceRecordText), $(ErpLicenceRecordAnchor)))</ErpLicenceRecordAttempted>
			<ErpLicenceRecordAccepted>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch($(ErpLicenceRecordText), $(ErpLicenceRecordShape.Replace('DECISIONTOKEN','accepted-rpl-1\.5'))))</ErpLicenceRecordAccepted>
			<ErpLicenceRecordDeclined>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch($(ErpLicenceRecordText), $(ErpLicenceRecordShape.Replace('DECISIONTOKEN','declined-rpl-1\.5'))))</ErpLicenceRecordDeclined>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded Condition="'$(ErpLicenceRecordAccepted)' == 'true'">accepted-rpl-1.5</ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded Condition="'$(ErpLicenceRecordDeclined)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpLicenceRecordAccepted)' != 'true'">declined-rpl-1.5</ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded>
		</PropertyGroup>
		<!-- Fail closed when the pin cannot be read, so the gate cannot be
		     disabled by moving or renaming the manifest it reads. -->
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)' == ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC004"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: the AutoMapper pin could not be read from '$(ErpCoreProjectFile)', so the licence-governance gate for CR2-F-04 cannot evaluate whether this package's declared PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' is accurate. This fails closed on purpose - a gate that cannot read its input must not pass. Restore the manifest, or update ErpCoreProjectFile in Directory.Build.props. See docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001)." />
		<!-- THE TRANSIENT FORM IS REFUSED, NOT IGNORED (GOV-01). Silently ignoring
		     a command-line decision would be worse than accepting it: the owner
		     would believe they had approved and would be told nothing. -->
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision)' != ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC005"
		       Text="ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision was supplied as an MSBuild property, and that form is no longer accepted as approval (review finding GOV-01: a transient 'dotnet pack -p:...' property is not durable authenticated approval - it records no approver, no date and no reviewable trace, and any script can supply it). Record the decision instead as one committed line in docs/security/risk-register.md: 'AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION: accepted-rpl-1.5 | approver: NAME AND CONTACT | date: YYYY-MM-DD | ref: TICKET OR URL'. Then pack with no property at all. See RISK-001 for both options and the reversal steps." />
		<!-- Fail closed when the record file itself cannot be read, for the same
		     reason ERPLIC004 fails closed on an unreadable pin. -->
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND !Exists('$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)')"
		       Code="ERPLIC006"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: the licence decision record file '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)' does not exist, so the owner's decision for CR2-F-04 cannot be read and this gate cannot evaluate whether $(MSBuildProjectName)'s declared PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' is accurate. This fails closed on purpose - a gate that cannot read its input must not pass. Restore the file, or update ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile in Directory.Build.props." />
		<!-- A contradiction is refused rather than resolved by precedence. -->
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpLicenceRecordAccepted)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpLicenceRecordDeclined)' == 'true'"
		       Code="ERPLIC007"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)' contains BOTH an accepted-rpl-1.5 and a declined-rpl-1.5 decision record. Two contradictory answers are not resolved by order or precedence here, because either choice would be this gate inventing the owner's intent. Leave exactly one record line and remove the other. See RISK-001." />
		<!-- Fail closed on a malformed answer, so a typo cannot read as consent. -->
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpLicenceRecordAttempted)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded)' == ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC003"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)' contains something shaped like an AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION record, but it does not match the required form, so it is refused rather than ignored - a typo must not be mistaken for the owner's consent. The line must read exactly: 'AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION: accepted-rpl-1.5 | approver: NAME AND CONTACT | date: YYYY-MM-DD | ref: TICKET OR URL', with declined-rpl-1.5 as the only other accepted answer, an ISO date, and no semicolons. See RISK-001." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded)' == ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC001"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: $(MSBuildProjectName) declares PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' while its dependency graph reaches AutoMapper $(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion), which is published under the Reciprocal Public License 1.5. Publishing that combination would state a licence this package does not carry, to every downstream consumer, irrevocably. This is a repository-owner decision (RISK-001) and is deliberately not settled automatically. To pack, commit ONE line to docs/security/risk-register.md - 'AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION: accepted-rpl-1.5 | approver: NAME AND CONTACT | date: YYYY-MM-DD | ref: TICKET OR URL' - so the approval is durable, attributable and reviewable; or decline it, revert the pin using the reversal steps in RISK-001, and pack normally. Build, publish and run are unaffected by this gate." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded)' == 'declined-rpl-1.5'"
		       Code="ERPLIC002"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: the Reciprocal Public License 1.5 is recorded as DECLINED in '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)', but AutoMapper $(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion) is still referenced, so $(MSBuildProjectName)'s declared licence expression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' would still be inaccurate. Complete the decline by reverting the pin as documented in docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001), which also restores advisory GHSA-rvv3-g6hj-g44x and therefore requires the narrow audit suppression and formal risk acceptance described there." />
		<Message Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded)' == 'accepted-rpl-1.5'"
		         Importance="high"
		         Text="CR2-F-04: packing $(MSBuildProjectName) with the Reciprocal Public License 1.5 recorded as ACCEPTED for AutoMapper $(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion), from the committed record in '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)'. Confirm PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' still states the intended posture before publishing. See docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001)." />
		<Message Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' != 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)' != ''"
		         Importance="high"
		         Text="CR2-F-04: no reciprocal-licence conflict detected for $(MSBuildProjectName) - AutoMapper resolves to a permissively licensed version, so no owner decision is required to pack." />
	</Target>

	<!-- ============================ SUPPRESSION SEAM ============================
	NOTHING IS SUPPRESSED HERE, and no advisory may be suppressed without a
	recorded, justified risk acceptance in docs/security/risk-register.md naming
	the advisory, its severity, why the upgrade is declined and who accepted the
	residual risk. Any such suppression must name ONE narrowly scoped diagnostic
	code - never a blanket NU19* wildcard, and never a wholesale disabling of the
	promotion above - or it defeats Validation Gate 2.

	Threat addressed by writing the seam out rather than describing it - review
	finding F7 (CWE-1059 insufficient documentation, applied to a security
	control). Four documents told an owner to come here and uncomment a sanctioned
	example; no example existed, so the only documented fallback for RISK-001 was
	not executable as written. An operator following it under time pressure would
	have improvised, and the shapes that come naturally to improvise -
	<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);$(NuGetAuditCodes)</NoWarn>, a NU19* wildcard, or deleting
	the promotion property group - each silence EVERY advisory in the graph rather
	than one. The two forms below are therefore recorded verbatim, inert, so the
	narrow shape is the one at hand.

	BOTH BLOCKS BELOW ARE INSIDE THIS COMMENT AND HAVE NO EFFECT. MSBuild never
	sees them; `dotnet msbuild -getProperty:NoWarn` on any project in this
	repository returns the SDK default with nothing appended by this file. They
	are a template to copy, not a switch to flip: uncommenting either one is the
	act that accepts residual risk, and it is invalid without the risk-register
	entry named above.

	FORM 1 - PREFERRED. NuGetAuditSuppress names ONE advisory by its URL, so the
	audit keeps reporting every other advisory at every severity, including a
	future one against the same package. This is the narrowest instrument
	available and is the form docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001,
	Option B) prescribes.

	It is NOT sufficient on its own, and the register records why: measured
	against a probe pinned to the vulnerable version, this item makes
	`dotnet restore` succeed but leaves the vulnerable-package listing
	(`dotnet list package` with the vulnerable and include-transitive switches)
	reporting the same High row, because that listing does not read
	NuGetAuditSuppress. The Gate 2 advisory step in
	.github/workflows/security-scan.yml therefore still fails, and RISK-001
	Option B carries the three edits that reconcile it.

	<ItemGroup>
		<NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rvv3-g6hj-g44x" />
	</ItemGroup>

	FORM 2 - FALLBACK ONLY, and strictly worse. NoWarn on NU1903 silences the
	high-severity audit CODE, which means it also hides any OTHER High advisory
	that appears later, in any package, in any project - a fail-open the moment
	the graph changes. Use it only if the toolchain in use predates
	NuGetAuditSuppress, and pair it with an explicit re-review date.

	<PropertyGroup>
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU1903</NoWarn>
	</PropertyGroup>

	Whichever form is used it must (1) sit outside this comment to take effect,
	(2) carry an inline justification naming the advisory and the risk-register
	entry, and (3) be negative-tested - remove it and confirm the restore fails
	again - because an untested suppression is indistinguishable from a gate that
	never worked.
	========================= END SUPPRESSION SEAM ========================== -->

</Project>
